Lab 5: Managing Neutron (Networking Service) in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure networks, subnets, and routers in OpenStack Neutron
Set up security groups and floating IPs for tenant isolation and external connectivity
Monitor and troubleshoot Neutron service status
Prerequisites
Access to an OpenStack cloud environment with admin privileges
OpenStack CLI tools installed (python-openstackclient)
Basic understanding of networking concepts (VLANs, subnets, routers)
Familiarity with Linux command line
Lab Setup
Ensure you have sourced your OpenStack RC file:
source ~/openrc.sh
Verify connectivity:
openstack network agent list
Expected Output: A list of active Neutron agents should appear.

Task 1: Configure Networks, Subnets, and Routers
Subtask 1.1: Create a Tenant Network
openstack network create --internal --provider-network-type vlan \
--provider-physical-network physnet1 \
--provider-segment 100 lab-network
Explanation:

--internal: Creates a network only accessible within the tenant
provider-network-type: Specifies VLAN isolation
physnet1: The physical network defined in neutron configuration
Verification:

openstack network show lab-network
Subtask 1.2: Create a Subnet
openstack subnet create --network lab-network \
--subnet-range 192.168.100.0/24 \
--gateway 192.168.100.1 \
--dns-nameserver 8.8.8.8 lab-subnet
Troubleshooting Tip: If you get DHCP errors, ensure the IP range doesn't conflict with existing networks.

Subtask 1.3: Create a Router
openstack router create lab-router
openstack router add subnet lab-router lab-subnet
Key Concept: Routers enable communication between different networks and provide NAT functionality.

Task 2: Security Groups and Floating IPs
Subtask 2.1: Configure Security Groups
# Create security group
openstack security group create lab-sg

# Add SSH rule
openstack security group rule create --proto tcp \
--dst-port 22 --remote-ip 0.0.0.0/0 lab-sg

# Add ICMP rule
openstack security group rule create --proto icmp lab-sg
Expected Outcome: These rules will allow SSH and ping traffic to instances.

Subtask 2.2: Allocate Floating IP
# Create floating IP
openstack floating ip create public

# Associate with instance (replace with actual instance ID)
openstack server add floating ip instance-1 <FLOATING_IP>
Monitoring: Verify association:

openstack server show instance-1
Task 3: Monitoring and Troubleshooting
Subtask 3.1: Check Neutron Service Status
openstack network agent list
Healthy State Indicators:

alive column should show :-)
All required agents (dhcp, l3, metadata) should be present
Subtask 3.2: Common Troubleshooting Commands
# Check network namespace
ip netns list

# Debug DHCP issues
openstack port list --network lab-network
Troubleshooting Flow:

Verify agent status
Check network connectivity between components
Review OpenStack and Neutron logs (/var/log/neutron)
Conclusion
In this lab, you have:

Created isolated tenant networks with proper subnet configuration
Implemented security policies through security groups
Enabled external connectivity using floating IPs
Monitored and troubleshooted Neutron services
Final Verification:

openstack network list
openstack subnet list
openstack router list
openstack security group list
